---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp";
const title = "Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify: compare the connection first";
const desc = "Compare Brevo PushOwl and Mailchimp for Shopify: data synchronization, audience ownership, campaign operations and total cost.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Brevo\u2019s Shopify connection just a contact import?",
    "answer": "The current guide describes Brevo PushOwl with catalog and order synchronization as well as customers. Confirm the app and workspace you will actually use, particularly when replacing a legacy integration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should an existing Mailchimp audience be reused?",
    "answer": "Audit its consent history, signup sources and active automations first. Reuse can preserve campaign assets, but subscription conflicts and duplicate welcome messages need resolving before activation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which is cheaper?",
    "answer": "Compare current quotes for identical contacts, sends, channels and support. Include integration charges and the effort of migrating templates and automations; this page supplies no fixed price estimate."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/compare">All comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">The first decision is which Shopify connection you will operate. Evaluate Brevo through its current PushOwl integration and Mailchimp through its Shopify audience integration before comparing editors or subscription quotes.</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Two different connection models</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s Shopify guide</a> describes Brevo PushOwl as combining email, SMS, web push and WhatsApp, with customer, product, collection and order synchronization. This makes the actual app and account relationship part of the buying decision. Establish which workspace owns campaigns, billing and customer records; do not assume a legacy Brevo setup matches the current instructions.</p>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s connection guide</a> connects a Shopify store to a Mailchimp audience and warns operators to pause existing welcome automations before connecting. For a team already maintaining newsletters there, retaining that audience can avoid creative migration. It also requires an audit of existing automations so the new connection does not enroll shoppers twice.</p>
<h2>What should decide the shortlist?</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Brevo evaluation</th><th>Mailchimp evaluation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Several messaging channels</td><td>Check which PushOwl channels your account and region can activate</td><td>Check available channels and whether the same audience governs them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Existing newsletter library</td><td>Estimate rebuilding templates and signup forms</td><td>Audit what can remain in the existing audience</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shopify product campaigns</td><td>Inspect synchronized catalog and order fields</td><td>Inspect product content and order-driven audience data</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service communications</td><td>Establish whether transactional sending belongs in this app or a separate Brevo setup</td><td>Inventory Shopify service notifications separately from marketing</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Proposed trial: one customer, conflicting subscription states</h2>
<p>Use controlled addresses owned by your team with sending restricted to those addresses. Create a new subscribed Shopify customer and an existing audience contact, then exercise unsubscribe and resubscribe paths. Inspect state in both systems after each change, recording direction and timing. The purpose is to determine which system can overwrite the other, rather than merely proving that a customer appears in a list.</p>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/mailchimp-shopify-syncing/">Mailchimp’s synchronization reference</a> documents an important case: an existing opted-in Mailchimp contact can update Shopify to subscribed regardless of the previous Shopify status. Review that behavior against your actual consent history before connecting an established audience. A successful import is not evidence that permission conflicts have been resolved.</p>
<h2>Campaign production and cart recovery</h2>
<p>Build a product announcement in each candidate using a variant with a changed price and a sold-out item. Ask the person who normally edits newsletters to replace the product and preview mobile content. Record manual work and stale catalog content. Familiarity is useful only if the current team can maintain the result.</p>
<p>Next propose a cart reminder, complete the purchase during its delay, and inspect whether the reminder is cancelled. Retry with a guest checkout and a returning customer using the same email. Keep Shopify’s existing recovery sender paused for the controlled exercise. Refund and service-case exclusions may require separate data sources; identify those gaps rather than assuming either integration supplies them automatically.</p>
<h2>Compare quotes for the same workload</h2>
<p>Request account-specific quotes using the same marketable customer count, campaign sends, automation sends and peak month. Separate SMS and other channel charges, seats, integrations and support. Confirm the relationship between Brevo and PushOwl charges instead of applying a generic Brevo plan to a Shopify app quote. For Mailchimp, check audience/contact treatment and the features needed for your actual automation branches.</p>
<p>Keep migration labor separate from recurring spend. A lower subscription can still be a poor move if every seasonal campaign requires rebuilding creative or repairing data. Conversely, keeping familiar templates has little value if subscription states cannot be reconciled safely.</p>
<h2>Cutover and decision</h2>
<p>Favor Brevo PushOwl when its confirmed channel scope solves a specific missing job and the team can operate the connected workspace. Favor staying with Mailchimp when its existing audience and campaign assets remain useful and the Shopify sync passes the consent and order tests. Neither is a universal cost winner.</p>
<p>Before activation, name one owner for each welcome, recovery and post-purchase sequence. Export consent evidence and suppression lists, pause the old sender, verify the replacement on controlled accounts, and retain rollback instructions. Continue with the <a class="link" href="/compare">comparison catalog</a> or <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase playbook</a>.</p>

</article></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before choosing" />
</BaseLayout>
